rsity in nashville lured by a  generous scholarship  the tennessee capitol is of course a  city of entertainers  the young king was drawn to the business  side of the industry  but he was also inspired by a television  show whose casting foreshadowed his  own work changing who was on the screen  this show that i used to watch with my  mother back in high school called  l.a law this light bulb went off in my  head this character played by blair  underwood this charismatic intelligent  african-american man and i i was like oh  like that blair underwood character that  could be really cool when i go to law  school  my life experiences growing up as a  young black man  in our country seeing some challenges  and issues that we're dealing with there  were some experiences i had during that  time that  also had an interest for me in civil  rights  and so i thought let me go to howard law  and and yet  you probably could have like you could  have been blair underwood or the blair  underwood character in many ways and  like gone to work for a big law firm  and you know sort of taken that path it  was clear for me too  that that wasn't the path for me the  summer after my second year i got a  clerkship  at mtv and one of the lawyers that i  worked with there said hey charles  i think you would make a really good  lawyer but you'd make a great agent  okay and i had no idea about the agency  world and i learned about all these  moguls in the industry  david geffen and barry diller mike ovitz  had just come out of caa and was  had this incredible gig at disney that  didn't work and  one his one year payoff was 95 million  so i was like wait a minute this  agency world and i thought that actually  could be a good fit for me  so king headed west as so many have in  search of a future in hollywood  with his then girlfriend and now wife  stacy  and also like many before and after him  fame and fortune  were not immediately forthcoming so this  is  like this is where it started this is  where i started when i came to la  i said let me start at the oldest  entertainment business in the industry  and at the time it's the william morris  agency  and that legendary agent training  program and they were located right here  in this building  i mean you are at at that point like  incredibly well  credentialed academically and the way  that the world worked  and probably still works to some extent  is you got to go work in the mail room  yes i mean what does that feel like at  that moment  it was it was tough it was very  challenging financially  you know 90 hours a week for very little  pay  at 100 000 in student loans i had to  defer them  there's a a big reason why they're  particularly at that time there are not  many people from diverse backgrounds  in there one it's hard to get in but two  financially it's  challenging to afford it there were no  african-american trainees when i started  there were a few assistants throughout  the building let's say if there were  two or three hundred assistants maybe  there were two wow  and but it was no different in any of  the other ways  that was just what the shape of the town  was the analogy i used to use of  what it was like coming up through the  ranks it's like working on the floor  of the stock exchange you know ticker  tape and people yelling and screaming  rolling phone calls you got four or five  lines going working with  and representing at times i mean up to  maybe like 100 artists so frankly  you know the the threshold of pain of  literally  constantly putting out fires and solving  a problem and i i can't think of a  better training ground  for what i'm doing now king didn't stay  in the mail room for long  quickly working his way up to assistant  then junior agent  just as many had before where he went  off script as it were  was with his client list whether it was  packaging the hit movie barbershop  or repping andre 3000 and dmx king was  becoming a king maker for black culture  i would share all of the statistics and  articles about the shifting demographics  and where we're going  and that you know hip-hop culture is  is youth culture and youth culture is  pop culture and everything is going in  that direction and these are the  demographics that are growing  and that the same thing is going to  happen in film and television that point  of view started to look pretty prescient  in 2005 when a couple of charles's film  clients  hit the big time after you know talking  about tyler perry for years in meetings  inviting people out to see his plays  we'd package his first movie dive with  mad black woman and  you know set up this unprecedented deal  for him at lionsgate to  co-finance and own the movie and that  small  film was you know a massive hit it made  like 50 million the box office and it  sold i don't know a ridiculous amount of  dvds  and it became the cornerstone foundation  of you know this incredible business  that tyler has built and so that  happened in 2005.  craig brewer was a young filmmaker that  i started representing about a month  before sundance that year he had this  movie called hustle and flow  feel that you know it's hard out here  for  up here hustle and flow went to sundance  and  that film made for three and a half  million became the biggest sale at  sundance that year  and then a few months after that we  started representing terrence howard who  got nominated for academy award that  later on that year  and uh yes i went from regular agent  2000 to  a senior vice president by 2008 who was  running our big motion picture meetings  as you as you kind of like this is a  good business it is absolutely and  you're representing some of the biggest  names in the world  and yet you say yeah but i want to do  this other thing  when i went into the mail room it was  with a vision of a 10-year plan to one  day be at the helm  of my own diversified entertainment and  media company i definitely knew  okay this vision that i have this is now  the time for me to begin to put the  put the pieces in place king left his  job to start macro in 2015  he was determined not only to create a  flourishing entertainment business  but to fulfill a social mission as well  centered around a radically and  historically underserved community of  creators and audiences  over time we created this thing called  the macro rubric and there's six key  points that we look for  at the center of every one of the  projects people of color must be at the  center of it either the lead  co-lead the driving through line and you  know people of color on the broad center  african-american latinx asian the wide  different demographic that i call of  the new majority and and so we  choose project one they have people of  color at the center of them projects  that have  a level of excellence and storytelling  and the other thing we of course look  for  in all of the years of experience is  we're looking at what is the business  opportunity for them we're going to take  some calculated risk  but here's where the upside opportunity  is for it  his stature within entertainment the  client list alone  would seem to make starting macro a  fairly low risk proposition  it wasn't he and his wife stacy  self-funded for six months while  investor after investor  passed it's not a slam dunk when you go  out to  to pitch it to people not at all were  you surprised it was  it was harrowing when i made the leap i  knew that there were going to be some  roadblocks and that there might be some  challenges  but that if anyone had the credentials  in the background to do it  that i stood about as strong a shot as  anyone  ever would up to that point how come you  ain't never liked me  like you who the hell said i got to like  you  macro's momentum really picked up when  they co-financed the movie fences in  2016.  starring denzel washington and viola  davis the adaptation of the august  wilson play helped define what macro  would be at its core  combining star power artistic  seriousness and commercial potential  and the oscar goes to viola davis  [Applause]  [Music]  but macros projects with their largely  black-centric stories and creators  would continue to run into challenges in  the marketplace  one striking example would be their  highly acclaimed gothic southern drama  mudbound in 2017.  if we could look at the uh laptop um i  want you to watch what uh  one of your one of your pals said about  a very specific movie  let's check it out the pal in question  ted sarandos the ceo of netflix nice  friend to have  our first deal really we did together um  was  uh mudbound i was in park city the last  couple of days of sundance  mudbound had not sold yet um my team had  i think had taken a pass on it early and  charles called me up and said ted i know  um i know that you would get it would  you  please do me a favor and see the movie  and i i said look there's not even a  screening charles i can't even screen it  anymore he goes no i'll get it to you  and brought over the screener for  mudbound i was able to watch it  and the next morning i was at the  breakfast table with dee reese making  the deal for mudbound  and did very well for us we're very very  proud of that work  and you think about that that little act  of charles knowing to call me  and him being right about it which he  always is um and mudbound became our the  first netflix original movie to be  nominated for an academy award  so that's a pretty good endorsement tell  me that story from your perspective  it's a very very real story we knew we  had a great film  and where we were with the offers from  the other  distributors was crickets things  weren't looking good and ted i just  called him up and said hey ted look you  know i don't reach out to you on a lot  of things but  i think this is one that will resonate  with you and then the rest was history  but the other thing that that movie show  when they did share the data with us  that movie was