anet  party it's gonna be a predominantly  black cast you know and you wanted to  come in with  you know in a position of power you know  what i mean and  that that charles was the only person we  could go to for that  this is history that people need to know  yeah and the only way that we can really  heal as a country  is by recognizing past issues and things  that have happened  and it was something that we felt like  compelled to get behind and yes there  was a level of  of risk to it but then we also saw the  upside opportunity  and the oscar goes to daniel kaluya  [Music]  i'd like to thank my team um incredible  support  charles king man everyone at macro  and how much does the oscar nominations  and all of that  sort of award buzz do for not just the  film but candidly for for the company i  mean  this feels like an inflection yes sort  of catalytic moment for macro yes  business-wise it's absolutely con  clearly  is opening up more doors we were already  you know doing well i mean we've had 15  academy award nominations for movies  we've either financed or produced  but i'd say now it's really going to be  about continuing that work but also  we're going to expand  we're building a company that i believe  within five to seven years from now will  be a billion plus  diversified entertainment and media  company  it's safe to say something is changing  in hollywood  from a more diverse slate of oscar  nominees to nbc ditching the golden  globes  to the commercial and critical success  of movies like judas and one night in  miami  not to mention the fierce anticipation  for black panther  sequel wakanda forever people of color  finally seem to be getting a seat at the  table in show business  not just creatively but on the business  side as well  i'd say we're getting to a place where  we're within the system  i still don't look at myself like uh  we're you know  one of the establishments or anything  like that but i do think that by our  success  it absolutely 